The baseline is a single plaintext-vector add, so the only cost is the plaintext encoding (~2.5 ms) and one ciphertext add. It needs no multiplications, rotations or rescales, and there is nothing left to remove from the circuit.

The one change I can try is the form of the operation. Writing `p + x0` or `x0 - (-p)` is normalized by CHEHAB into the same circuit, so it would not count as a new circuit. I'll submit the equivalent kernel with the operands swapped, accepting that it will probably match the baseline and not win.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext p("p");
  Ciphertext y = x0 + p;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The circuit is already a single add with plaintext encoding, so there is no cheaper circuit. Any variant that changes the circuit would add operations, because every extra operation costs more than it saves. One possible gain is the plaintext encoding at ~2.5 ms. It depends on the input itself, and the DSL gives no way to skip it, since a plaintext input must be encoded to be added.

I'm resubmitting the minimal kernel. I expect it to match the baseline and can't offer a cheaper circuit.

